Project scaffolding

The repository was initially scaffolded from https://github.com/PaulRBerg/foundry-template.
Implementations of the IBC specification in Solidity
(https://github.com/hyperledger-labs/yui-ibc-solidity), CosmWasm
(https://github.com/srdtrk/cw-ibc-lite), and Rust (https://github.com/cosmos/ibc-rs) were
consulted as references. No file in this repository is currently identified as copied from any
of these.


Open items

This record is complete as to the provenance that is documented in the source tree. Three things
are not settled, and are listed here rather than left implied:

  1. The Open Ethereum GPL-3.0-or-later code in packages/ethereum/trie-db/ is an unresolved
     license incompatibility, not a completed attribution. See that entry above.

  2. The adapted-versus-original boundary within packages/ethereum/ has not been established
     file by file. The light-client and apis crates record their derivation from Union in their
     READMEs; packages/ethereum/types does not, although it sits in the same light client and
     may share provenance. Establishing the boundary is what would let the union entry above
     name specific files rather than whole directories.

  3. Where an upstream is under the MIT License or a BSD license, that license requires its
     copyright and permission notice be retained verbatim in copies or substantial portions.
     The entries above are Apache-2.0 or GPL upstreams, for which the reproduced license
     reference is sufficient. If any MIT-licensed code — including from the foundry-template
     scaffolding or an npm dependency whose source is checked in later — is found to have been
     copied into this repository, its verbatim notice must be added here at that point.

Separately, the repository was normalized to Apache-2.0 across files previously marked MIT or
UNLICENSED. That relicensing was carried out without per-contributor sign-off and is a matter
for legal rather than for this file, but it is noted here because MIT permits sublicensing only
where the original copyright notice is preserved.


